# `=` in a `join ... on` condition: DS0110.
mode: syntax
---- query
'orders.csv' | join 'customers.csv' on $left.customerId = $right.customerId
---- expected
PROGRAM@0..76
  PIPELINE@0..75
    LITERAL@0..12
      STRING@0..12 "'orders.csv'"
    WHITESPACE@12..13 " "
    PIPE@13..14 "|"
    WHITESPACE@14..15 " "
    JOIN_OP@15..75
      IDENT@15..19 "join"
      WHITESPACE@19..20 " "
      LITERAL@20..35
        STRING@20..35 "'customers.csv'"
      WHITESPACE@35..36 " "
      IDENT@36..38 "on"
      WHITESPACE@38..39 " "
      BIN_EXPR@39..75
        FIELD_EXPR@39..55
          NAME_REF@39..44
            DOLLAR_LEFT@39..44 "$left"
          DOT@44..45 "."
          NAME_REF@45..55
            IDENT@45..55 "customerId"
        WHITESPACE@55..56 " "
        EQ@56..57 "="
        WHITESPACE@57..58 " "
        FIELD_EXPR@58..75
          NAME_REF@58..64
            DOLLAR_RIGHT@58..64 "$right"
          DOT@64..65 "."
          NAME_REF@65..75
            IDENT@65..75 "customerId"
  WHITESPACE@75..76 "\n"

error[DS0110]: `=` is assignment, not comparison
  --> query:1:57
  |
1 | 'orders.csv' | join 'customers.csv' on $left.customerId = $right.customerId
  |                                                         ^ assignment is not allowed here
  |
  = help: use `==` to compare values: `$left.customerId == $right.customerId`

with the machine-applicable fixes applied:
    'orders.csv' | join 'customers.csv' on $left.customerId == $right.customerId
which parses without errors
